A||||4||Oreif||Big Bore Throttle Body for V-6 results!!!||||2||
Z||000000||Oreif||04-29-2000||02:57 PM||||OK here it goes, First I did not dyno the car. The results were optained by  use of a digital stopwatch. I ran from 0 to 60 mph 5 times and took the average. It is an 86 SE V-6 with sprint manifolds, Accel ignition, shift kit in auto trans, and bosch platnum plugs.<BR>Before Throttle body. Average 0-60 was 6.85 seconds. Best time was 6.81. Since it's an automatic it was gas and SW button until speedo hit 60.<BR>Installed new throttle body from Fiero Store (3mm bigger than stock) minor porting on manifold side was required. 0-60 times are now 6.69 average of 5 runs. best time being 6.67. So if it is true 1/10 of a sec is 10HP<BR>I have a gain of about 15hp. Best guess is about 10-11% gain. Low end torque seems about the same (calibrated A$$-O-Meter) but once the RPM's get over around 2000-2500 it does seem to "breathe" better. (again calibrated A$$-O-Meter)<BR>I would guess a .02 error for reaction between SW button and gas pedal.<P>The throttle body costs $199 (+core) at the Fiero Store. Is 10% more HP equal to $199? that is the question you will have to answer yourself. I like the results I got and it only took about 45 minutes to swap!||||reg||2
Z||000001||Shaun41178||04-29-2000||03:57 PM||||Cool an actual calibrated @ss-meter.  Many peoples meters here are out of adjustment.  What did you use to correct yours?<P>Using a stopwatch is not the best way to measure changes in acceleration.  The human body is not as accurate as a true device that is designed to do this kind of thing i.e: G-Tech.  i had a freind sit in my car on a nitrous run of mine with a stopwatch and he said I clicked off to 60 in 8 secs.  Hooked my g-tech and was doing it in 6.5.  A factor that plays an intricate role in timing o-60 is the speedo.  If yor speedo is a couple mph off then it will not give an accurate reading.  Also seeing the speedo hit 60 the signal going to your brain then interpretted and the signal to get down to your finger to hit the stop button and then the button being pushed down can add up to about a .1 of a second.  I just say get a g-tech and get true readings.  you did a good job the way you did by taking the averages of the runs.  But a g-tech won't need the average and will get it right the first time.<P>I had a ported TB borrowed from Mike Lecompte dynoed on my 85 Gt as his 86 GT was out of commission.  Only the TB was ported and the intake plenum was not touched.  I picked up a maximum of about 4 hp.  It is between 3-4 I got and about 3-4 torque.  (I did make a post about this several months ago)  However power increased throughout the entire rpm range.  With a ported intake upper plenum I would think power would go up 1-2 hp.  No 15 hp gain from this mod though.  Sorry.<P>1/10 of a second equates to ROUGHLY 10hp but that is 1/10 in the QTR MILE not to 60.  1/10 to 60 equates to about .02-.04 in the qtr mile.  Which means you only picked up about 5 or so hp.  <P>Sorry to  burst your bubble<P>Shaun||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||04-29-2000||04:08 PM||||This throttle body, is it the stock one bored out?||||reg||5
Z||000003||Oreif||04-29-2000||05:36 PM||||Shaun, You didn't burst my bubble but thanks for explaining the 1/10 to HP. Well at least even with brain response and finger response I at least proved that there was an actual change in performance. I don't know anyone with a Gtech but would be willing to try one to see what the real numbers are. I was thinking of having it dyno'd this summer to get real HP/torque readings too.<BR>WKDFIERO, The throttle body is a stock fiero TB that is bored out 3mm by the Fiero Store. They also taper the edge and re-machine the IAC port. Holding a stock and bored out TB next to each other you can see that the flat edge is about half the thickness from a sock one. (looking at the manifold side of the TB).<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||mwbackus||04-29-2000||06:53 PM||||For those that are interested in a ported throttle body, I have archived notes from a person that performs this service:<P>Mike Morse   <A HREF="mailto:MORSE86@aol.com">MORSE86@aol.com</A> <P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I disassemble your throttle body, glass bead the outside surfaces, Clean the inside of the casting, bolt it to a special fixture in the lathe, and bore the inside diameter to 56 mm. Then the throttle shaft is modified to accept a custom made aluminum throttle plate. (Same material as stock) The throttle body is deburred, cleaned, and reassembled with new screws (peened to prevent them from loosening). Some times I have to drill and retap the TPS mounting bosses to replace the stock aluminum screws, as they tend to corrode and twist off upon removal in the die cast housing.<BR>The plenum is bolted to a special fixture that gets mounted on a vertical milling machine, then it is indicated in, so it is in the proper location to bore the opening to match the throttle body.<BR>Then I do a little hand blending with a die grinder as needed.<BR>Prices are as follows:<BR>Modify your throttle body and plenum together, $150.00 including return<BR>shipping, (one week turn around upon receiving parts).<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Mike B.<P>||||reg||1
